==================================================
2. CORE DESIGN DIRECTION
==================================================

The website must feel like:

QUIET CLINICAL LUXURY

Think:

- premium private dental practice
- editorial healthcare design
- sophisticated clinical credibility
- calm confidence
- refined typography
- excellent spacing
- excellent photography
- strong hierarchy
- restrained interaction
- high perceived quality

The design should feel intentional and expensive without becoming flashy.

Do NOT turn the website into:
- a SaaS dashboard
- a generic medical template
- a startup landing page
- a luxury fashion website
- an AI-generated website
- an over-animated portfolio

==================================================
3. EXISTING DESIGN SYSTEM — PRESERVE AND REFINE
==================================================

Preserve the existing design direction established in Phases 1–3.5.

Typography:
- Plus Jakarta Sans for body/UI
- Cormorant Garamond for display headings

Palette:
- Warm Ivory / off-white
- Deep Navy / ink
- Restrained Gold
- Muted clinical blue/grey

Existing approximate tokens:
- #F9F8F6
- #F4F3F1
- #013565
- #0A2E58
- #C8A96E

Shapes:
- rectangular
- restrained 2px–4px radii
- no excessive rounding

Maintain:
- strong grid
- editorial spacing
- clear alignment
- deliberate proportions
- strong contrast
- restrained borders
- subtle shadows

Do NOT introduce a new design system.

==================================================
4. PREMIUM UI/UX REFINEMENT
==================================================

Go through the COMPLETE website and refine the interface so that every page feels like part of the same premium practice.

Routes include:

/
 /about
 /our-dentist
 /services
 /services/general-dentistry
 /services/implant-dentistry
 /services/cosmetic-dentistry
 /services/orthodontics
 /services/restorative-dentistry
 /services/childrens-dentistry
 /services/laser-teeth-whitening
 /testimonials
 /faqs
 /contact
 /book-an-appointment

Do not redesign randomly page-by-page.

Establish consistency across the entire experience.

--------------------------------------------------
4A. TYPOGRAPHY
--------------------------------------------------

Refine typography hierarchy across every page.

Check:

- H1 scale
- H2 scale
- H3 scale
- eyebrow/overline text
- body text
- supporting text
- metadata
- navigation
- buttons
- form labels
- helper text

Headings should have editorial presence without becoming oversized.

Cormorant Garamond should be used deliberately for major display headings.

Plus Jakarta Sans should remain highly readable for UI and body content.

Avoid:
- excessive italic serif text
- repeated decorative serif phrases
- tiny body text
- overly wide text blocks
- poor line lengths
- excessive font-weight variation

Use:
- `text-wrap: balance` / `text-pretty` where appropriate
- appropriate `max-width`
- strong vertical rhythm

--------------------------------------------------
4B. SPACING
--------------------------------------------------

Perform a full spacing consistency pass.

Look for:

- sections too close together
- sections with excessive empty space
- inconsistent horizontal padding
- inconsistent vertical rhythm
- headings too close to paragraphs
- buttons too close to text
- cards/rows with inconsistent internal padding

Create a coherent spacing rhythm using the existing Tailwind system.

Do not solve spacing problems with random one-off pixel values unless genuinely necessary.

Do not introduce breakpoint-specific hacks.

--------------------------------------------------
4C. BUTTONS AND CTAs
--------------------------------------------------

Refine every CTA.

Buttons should feel:

- substantial
- crisp
- premium
- accessible
- intentional

Maintain rectangular geometry with restrained radius.

Ensure:

- minimum comfortable touch target
- strong contrast
- clear hover state
- clear active state
- clear keyboard focus
- disabled state where applicable
- no hover-only meaning

Use subtle transitions based primarily on:
- color
- opacity
- transform where appropriate

Do NOT use:
- giant pills
- glowing buttons
- gradient buttons
- animated borders
- excessive bounce
- cursor-following effects

Primary CTA and secondary CTA should have clearly differentiated hierarchy.

--------------------------------------------------
4D. NAVIGATION
--------------------------------------------------

Refine Header and navigation.

Ensure:

- excellent desktop spacing
- clean services dropdown
- predictable keyboard navigation
- strong focus states
- mobile menu feels premium
- active/current route is identifiable
- menu transitions are subtle
- header does not feel oversized
- sticky behavior does not consume excessive viewport height

Do not introduce a huge floating header.

Ensure the logo remains crisp and correctly proportioned.

--------------------------------------------------
4E. CARDS / EDITORIAL ROWS
--------------------------------------------------

Review every card and editorial row.

Avoid generic:
"three cards with icon + title + text"

Prefer the existing editorial language:

- ruled rows
- numbered services
- strong typography
- restrained imagery
- intentional borders
- asymmetric layouts where appropriate
- clear hierarchy

Where cards are genuinely useful, make them feel editorial rather than component-library generic.

--------------------------------------------------
4F. IMAGERY
--------------------------------------------------

Preserve the complete Phase 3.5 asset mapping.

ALL 15 supplied assets must remain meaningfully used.

Do NOT remove images simply to simplify the UI.

Do NOT move the doctor portrait into the homepage hero.

Doctor portrait remains:
`/our-dentist`

Homepage hero remains:
clinic/exterior imagery.

Use imagery with:

- correct aspect ratio
- appropriate crop
- high-quality presentation
- consistent visual treatment
- meaningful alt text

Avoid:
- arbitrary image crops
- stretched images
- low-quality rendering
- excessive rounded image containers
- decorative image clutter

==================================================
5. MOTION AND MICRO-INTERACTIONS
==================================================

The site should feel alive, but quiet.

Use motion only where it improves:

- orientation
- feedback
- hierarchy
- perceived quality

Prefer:

- opacity
- transform
- subtle height transitions
- small translate distances
- controlled duration
- easing

Review existing Framer Motion usage.

Remove unnecessary animation.

Do NOT add animation everywhere.

Do NOT introduce:
- cursor tracking
- magnetic buttons
- scroll-jacking
- parallax-heavy effects
- floating particles
- glowing effects
- excessive stagger animations
- continuous animations

Interactive elements should have meaningful states:

- hover
- focus-visible
- active
- disabled where applicable

==================================================
6. REDUCED MOTION
==================================================

Audit the entire application for reduced-motion support.

When:

`prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`

animations should be:

- disabled
- shortened
- or reduced to immediate state changes

Ensure no essential information depends on animation.

Do not create separate broken layouts for reduced motion.

==================================================
7. PERFORMANCE HARDENING
==================================================

Improve performance without changing the visual architecture.

IMPORTANT:

Do NOT make every image or video eager.

Do NOT undo the Phase 3.5 hero-loading strategy.

Do NOT preload every asset.

--------------------------------------------------
7A. IMAGES
--------------------------------------------------

Audit every image.

Use `next/image` where appropriate.

Ensure:

- correct width/height or aspect ratio
- responsive `sizes`
- appropriate loading behavior
- meaningful priority only for true above-the-fold imagery
- no unnecessary preloads

Hero:
- preload/priority only the initial hero image required for first paint
- subsequent slideshow images must remain non-blocking

Below-the-fold:
- lazy load appropriately

Do not use huge source images when a smaller optimized asset is sufficient.

Check for unnecessarily large assets and improve delivery where possible without degrading visible quality.

--------------------------------------------------
7B. FONTS
--------------------------------------------------

Audit font loading.

Ensure:

- local fonts are used correctly
- no unnecessary external font requests
- no duplicate font loading
- appropriate font-display behavior
- only required weights/styles are loaded

Do not replace the existing fonts.

--------------------------------------------------
7C. JAVASCRIPT
--------------------------------------------------

Reduce unnecessary client-side JavaScript.

Audit:

- `"use client"`
- Framer Motion
- unnecessary state
- unnecessary effects
- browser-only logic
- event listeners

Prefer Server Components where interactivity is not required.

Do not rewrite architecture unnecessarily.

--------------------------------------------------
7D. HYDRATION
--------------------------------------------------

Look for components that can remain server-rendered.

Avoid hydration where it provides no value.

Do not introduce hydration mismatch risks.

==================================================
8. ACCESSIBILITY HARDENING
==================================================

Perform a complete accessibility implementation pass.

Target strong practical WCAG 2.2 AA behavior.

--------------------------------------------------
8A. SEMANTIC STRUCTURE
--------------------------------------------------

Ensure:

- one meaningful H1 per page
- logical heading hierarchy
- semantic `header`
- `nav`
- `main`
- `section`
- `footer`
- lists where content is actually a list
- buttons are buttons
- links are links

Do not use divs as interactive controls.

--------------------------------------------------
8B. KEYBOARD ACCESS
--------------------------------------------------

Every interactive element must be keyboard accessible.

Check:

- navigation
- services dropdown
- mobile menu
- FAQ accordion
- appointment form
- buttons
- links
- social links
- WhatsApp CTA
- form controls

Ensure focus is visible.

Do not remove browser focus indicators without providing a better replacement.

--------------------------------------------------
8C. FOCUS MANAGEMENT
--------------------------------------------------

For mobile menus and interactive overlays:

- manage focus appropriately
- allow Escape to close where appropriate
- prevent inaccessible background interaction where required
- restore focus logically after closing

Do not over-engineer.

--------------------------------------------------
8D. FORMS
--------------------------------------------------

Audit appointment/contact forms.

Ensure:

- labels are properly associated
- required fields are communicated
- validation errors are understandable
- errors are programmatically associated where appropriate
- keyboard navigation works
- focus moves appropriately after submission errors
- loading/submission state is communicated
- success state is understandable
- buttons are not ambiguous

Preserve existing validation and API behavior.

Do NOT break:
- Zod validation
- rate limiting
- API routes
- appointment flow
- existing security controls

--------------------------------------------------
8E. IMAGES
--------------------------------------------------

Ensure meaningful alt text.

Decorative images should be correctly marked decorative.

Do not put meaningless filenames into alt attributes.

--------------------------------------------------
8F. COLOR / CONTRAST
--------------------------------------------------

Audit text and interactive states for adequate contrast.

Especially:

- muted grey text
- gold text
- navy backgrounds
- footer
- buttons
- form helper text
- focus indicators

Do not sacrifice accessibility for the aesthetic.

==================================================
9. SEO HARDENING
==================================================

Audit the complete metadata architecture.

Every indexable page should have:

- unique title
- unique meta description
- appropriate canonical URL
- appropriate Open Graph metadata
- appropriate Twitter/X metadata where supported
- correct robots behavior

Do not duplicate generic metadata across every page.

--------------------------------------------------
9A. TITLES
--------------------------------------------------

Create meaningful page-specific titles.

Examples of structure:

Gilgal Dental Clinics | [Page Topic] | Ikoyi, Lagos

Do not keyword-stuff.

--------------------------------------------------
9B. DESCRIPTIONS
--------------------------------------------------

Write concise, natural descriptions based only on verified business information.

Do not invent:

- awards
- rankings
- patient counts
- success rates
- years not confirmed
- certifications
- testimonials

--------------------------------------------------
9C. CANONICALS
--------------------------------------------------

Ensure canonical URLs resolve correctly.

Use the site's configured production URL consistently.

Do not create conflicting canonical URLs.

--------------------------------------------------
9D. OPEN GRAPH
--------------------------------------------------

Ensure important pages have sensible OG metadata.

Use appropriate existing brand imagery.

Do not create fake OG imagery containing fabricated claims.

==================================================
10. STRUCTURED DATA
==================================================

Audit existing structured data.

Implement or correct appropriate schema where supported by verified information.

Potentially relevant schema:

- DentalClinic / Dentist
- LocalBusiness
- Organization
- WebSite
- BreadcrumbList
- FAQPage where appropriate and actually represented on the page

Do not add schema merely for SEO manipulation.

Every structured-data claim must match visible/verified site information.

Do not fabricate:
- ratings
- review counts
- prices
- opening hours
- credentials
- awards

